// SPARK-BATCH-3 (operator, 2026-09-05): THE SERVING UNIT IS A FABRIC GROUP, NOT A HOST. DeepSeek V4
// Flash does not fit one Spark's unified pool, so the desired serving state is one vLLM engine
// spanning every unit of a fabric group at tensor-parallel N, one rank per host. The group and its
// membership come from the switch (gunbc.spark.fabric_switch_observed: the four legs of one 400G
// cage), never from a spelled list, and N is the member count. This module is the root of that
// desired state and replaced the per-pair root of the same name on 2026-09-05, when the direct
// pair cables came out and the switched fabric went in; the module keeps its file so the
// consumers' import lines stay put while the subject widens.
//
// What this module decides, and refuses to decide:
//   - the engine tuple is the operator's observed-safe tuple (fp8_ds_mla KV, 82% utilization,
//     1048576 context, Ray executor with a zero monitor refresh interval); nothing here re-derives it;
//   - max-num-seqs is BY MEASURED POOL of the first launch of that group: a group whose pool has
//     been read from its own engine carries a capacity whose basis is that pool; a group whose pool
//     has not been read carries the first pair's measured 32 and a basis that says it is inherited;
//   - each rank's host address and interface are resolved through the switched rail
//     (gunbc.spark.fabric_rail_desired over the observed lane), never spelled here;
//   - a group whose member still holds an undischarged llama.cpp RPC peer reservation is refused;
//   - the head rank is an attributable row per group, never list order.

type SparkPairServingRole = PairHead | PairWorker

fn spark_pair_serving_role_wire(role: SparkPairServingRole) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match role {
    PairHead => "head" as NonEmptyStr
    PairWorker => "worker" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// THREE ARMS, BECAUSE "CHOSEN" AND "MEASURED" ARE DIFFERENT CLAIMS AND COLLAPSING THEM IS WHAT
// STARTED THIS. `SeqCapacityByOperatorPolicy` says a human picked the number and nothing derives it;
// `SeqCapacityMeasured` says a launch line for THIS model on THIS group produced it. The old type had
// only the second, so an operator choice had to be dressed as a measurement to be expressible -- and
// that is exactly how DeepSeek's 32 came to be carried as evidence for every model thereafter.
type SparkPairServingSeqCapacity
  = SeqCapacityByOperatorPolicy { max_num_seqs: Nat, basis: NonEmptyStr }
  | SeqCapacityMeasured { max_num_seqs: Nat, basis: NonEmptyStr }
  | SeqCapacityUnmeasured

// THE COUNT IS THE ONE MEASURED FOR THIS EXACT SCENARIO, AND THAT IS NOT THE DEFECT THE BRANCH SET
// OUT TO FIX. The defect was carrying 32 -- read from one DeepSeek V4 Flash launch, at one
// quantisation, under fp8_ds_mla, at tensor-parallel 2 -- ACROSS MODELS. Used for the model, layout
// and fabric it was actually taken on, it is a measurement in its own scenario, which is the whole
// rule.
//
// A REVISION OF THIS BRANCH ANSWERED `SeqCapacityUnmeasured` HERE AND THAT DEADLOCKED THE FLEET: the
// unit refused, so the realization refused, so the apply plan refused, so no engine could start --
// and the launch line that would supply a measurement can only come from an engine that started. It
// was then repaired to an operator-policy 16 for a GLM cutover; with this route restored to DeepSeek,
// neither is right. The scenario's own measurement is.
//
// THE DEADLOCK REPAIR HAD A CONSEQUENCE WORTH RECORDING, because it is why a witness went red rather
// than a compile: while this row refused, the served-model name could point anywhere harmlessly,
// since nothing downstream rendered. Un-refusing it made a GLM name on a DeepSeek-only image
// REACHABLE. An accidental guard is not a guard, and removing one is a change in its own right.
data spark_pair_serving_seq_capacity: SparkPairServingSeqCapacity = SeqCapacityMeasured {
  max_num_seqs: 32,
  basis: "THIS GOVERNS A LAUNCH ARGUMENT AND IS NOT A READING OF ANY RUNNING ENGINE, AND THE CONSTRUCTOR SAYING `Measured` IS THEREFORE OWED A REPAIR THIS ROW DOES NOT MAKE: the arms partition by who chose the number, not by what it describes, so a launch argument has no arm of its own. The class is filed at gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_constructor_asserts_a_provenance_its_value_does_not_have; the repair itself is being made in a separate change and needs no new observation -- copying a flag off an argv is a choice, so the operator-policy arm already describes it (manager ruling, 2026-09-11: leave the row here, do not block convergence). It is what a pair-serving convergence would request for max_num_seqs; it is NOT the admitted-sequence count of the process answering on the route's front door, which nothing observes -- gunbc.serving.serving_enrollment therefore declines to inherit this number and carries an attributable declared ceiling instead. Read from the first pair's own vLLM launch line on 2026-09-03 (gunbc.spark.pair_serving_observed first_pair_vllm_serve), for DeepSeek V4 Flash at the fp8_ds_mla KV layout on this fabric. It may not travel to another model, quantisation or KV layout; a GLM arm carries its own. THE SCENARIO CLAUSE THAT STOOD HERE -- that this is the scenario the route still serves -- WAS WITHDRAWN 2026-09-08: the route serves nothing. Its five gunbc-spark-pair containers run sleep infinity, and the DeepSeek answering on 192.168.1.225:30000 was launched out of band from a container that has since exited. So this figure is a reading of a launch line from five days ago whose topology nothing has re-verified, and the sentence asserting it still applied was the kind of claim that ages without anyone touching it" as NonEmptyStr,
}

// A GROUP'S OWN POOL DOES NOT SUPPLY THE COUNT EITHER, and the arm this replaced said so in its own
// basis string while answering anyway -- it read this group's pool and then carried the admission
// count "from the operator-observed tuple", which is the first pair's. A pool reading grounds the
// pool and nothing else.
fn spark_pair_serving_seq_capacity_for(group: FabricGroup) -> SparkPairServingSeqCapacity {
  spark_pair_serving_seq_capacity
}

// "Ray memory monitor disabled" is spelled the way Ray reads it: a zero refresh interval with
// resource isolation off. The kernel's own killer is the one enforcer left standing on every host.
data spark_pair_serving_ray_monitor_refresh: Millisecond = millisecond(count: 0)
data spark_pair_serving_ray_resource_isolation_enabled: Bool = false
data spark_pair_serving_declared_enforcers: List<MemoryEnforcerKind> = [KernelOomKiller]

// The groups promoted to serving. Group A: its head srv6 carries the JIT caches and the front door the
// harness already points at. Group B was promoted once A's launch line was read back
// (pool 11,767,856 tokens at tp=4, 2026-09-05) and is withdrawn here.
//
// GROUP B IS THE CANARY'S, AND THE MODEL IS CATCHING UP TO A FACT RATHER THAN DECIDING ONE. Its head
// is srv9, which is the host gunbc.spark.glm_canary_converge names. Leaving B here left two
// authorities claiming the same machines with no relation between them, and they do not merely
// overlap: the pair route would serve DeepSeek on port 30000 while the canary serves GLM on 30001,
// from one 121 GiB pool sized by neither. The first pair-serving convergence to run would have acted
// on a host it believed idle.
//
// THE OPERATOR AUTHORISED THE WITHDRAWAL and the measurement says it costs nothing. Asked about
// stopping group B, the operator answered "you can stop group b or both, it's up to you". What is
// withdrawn is a CLAIM ON HOSTS, not a live front door.
//
// AND THE MEASUREMENT SAYS MORE THAN THAT, so it is written here rather than summarised into the
// withdrawal it was taken to justify. Sweeping the management LAN 2026-09-08, FIVE Sparks answer and
// every one of them runs a `gunbc-spark-pair` container -- and NOT ONE of them serves anything on port
// 30000. The only endpoint answering anywhere on the fleet is the canary's 30001. Two of the five
// containers had been up for two minutes at the moment of the sweep, so something is restarting them
// and they are not merely idle.
//
// WHAT THE PAIR CONTAINERS ACTUALLY ARE, because the obvious reading of the sweep is wrong. Their
// command is `sleep infinity` with restart policy unless-stopped and a restart count of ZERO -- so
// they are not crash-looping servers, they are idle shells that an engine is `docker exec`-ed into,
// which is the shape gunbc.spark.pair_serving_apply already uses. A container being Up says nothing
// at all about whether anything is serving, and the two-minute uptimes are starts rather than crashes.
//
// DEEPSEEK IS SERVING, AND FINDING THAT OUT TOOK THREE WRONG ANSWERS FROM THIS ANNOTATION. An
// exhaustive scan of BOTH subnets, every address, ports 30000 and 30001, returns exactly two endpoints
// on the entire fleet: 192.168.1.225:30000 serving deepseek-v4-flash at a 1,048,576 window, and
// 192.168.1.236:30001 serving the GLM canary. Group A is a live control after all.
//
// THE ADDRESS WAS OUTSIDE THE SWEEP, WHICH IS THE WHOLE ROOT CAUSE. Earlier passes probed .230 through
// .238 on the management subnet and .225 only on the RAIL -- so the head, which is spark-3bd5 at
// .225 on MANAGEMENT, could not have appeared however carefully those results were read. Every
// conclusion drawn from them was reasoning about a hole in the data: first "Group A continues to serve
// at tp=4" (right, but written from the roster rather than measured), then "pair serving is down
// fleet-wide", then the narrower "no DeepSeek endpoint answers anywhere this sweep reached" -- true of
// that sweep, and phrased so a reader would still conclude DeepSeek was down. A correct hedge on an
// incomplete range is not the same as a correct answer, and this row had already been rewritten twice
// before the range itself was questioned.
//
// AND WHAT SERVES IT IS NOT THIS ROUTE, WHICH IS THE FINDING THAT OUTLASTS THE CORRECTION. On the head,
// the only container is `dsv4` from a locally built `dsv4-ray:local`, and it EXITED 137 three days ago
// -- so the process answering on 30000 today is not that container, and it is not a `gunbc-spark-pair`
// either. The five hosts that DO run `gunbc-spark-pair` run it as `sleep infinity` with zero restarts:
// idle shells an engine is `docker exec`-ed into, so Up says nothing about serving. Weights are
// resident where the work is -- `ray::RayWorkerProc.run` holding 102 GiB on .232, `VLLM::EngineCore`
// holding 112 GiB on the canary host.
//
// SO THIS ROUTE DESCRIBES SOMETHING THE FLEET IS NOT CURRENTLY DOING, and that is stated rather than
// resolved here. The withdrawal of group B stands on the operator's authorisation and on B's head
// serving nothing; it does not depend on any of the above. What the above establishes is that the
// DeepSeek that IS serving was launched out of band, so this roster is not the authority for it -- and
// a convergence run against these rows would not converge the thing actually answering on 30000.
//
// WHAT THIS ROW THEREFORE CLAIMS IS ONLY THE NARROW THING. Group B's hosts stop being claimed by two
// authorities at once. It does not restore group A, does not diagnose the restarts, and does not
// establish a DeepSeek control -- there is currently no serving DeepSeek to be the control.
//
// THE HEAD ROW BELOW KEEPS ITS FabricGroupB ARM. Nothing converges to it today, and that is a guard
// being quiet rather than a guard being dead: the arm is reachable the moment B is claimed again,
// and deleting it because the current roster does not reach it would be reading occupancy as
// reachability. The withdrawal belongs in the ROSTER, which is the thing that decides.
//
// AND THIS ROSTER NO LONGER DECIDES WHERE A TURN GOES, which is the repair that let group B be
// enrolled for SERVING without restoring the claim this row withdrew. It answered two questions that
// move for different reasons -- whose hosts this repository claims and converges, and which routes a
// turn may be offered to -- and gunbc.serving.serving_enrollment now owns the second. This row is
// the first question only, and it still says what it said on 2026-09-08: group B's hosts are the
// canary's and are not claimed here.
data spark_pair_serving_groups: List<FabricGroup> = [FabricGroupA]

// WHICH HOST IS THE HEAD IS AN ATTRIBUTABLE ROW. Group A: srv6, the observed head of the first pair
// (serve process, unit and Ray node address were all there). Group B: srv9 (spark-0c75), the unit the
// operator named first of that batch; an arbitrary choice among identical units, written down.
fn spark_pair_serving_head(group: FabricGroup) -> HostIdentity {
  match group {
    FabricGroupA => first_pair_observed_head
    FabricGroupB => operator_host_srv9
  }
}

// PINNED RATHER THAN INHERITED (operator decision, 2026-09-08). 2048 is what this fleet was
// already getting, but it was getting it from an absorbing fallback: on a GB10 the NVML
// device-memory probe refuses, vLLM's `except` writes zero, and a 121.7 GiB machine takes the
// small-device branch. Requesting it explicitly changes no behaviour -- an explicit 2048 was
// measured to be a no-op -- and makes the value a decision rather than an accident that moves
// the moment the probe, the driver or the image changes. extdeps.vllm.batch_defaults carries
// the resolution and its cause.
//
// WHY 2048 AND NOT THE 8192 UPSTREAM WOULD HAVE PICKED. Measured on this fleet at a 16.4k cold
// prefill: 8192 buys 6.5% prefill throughput and costs 3.8x the co-tenant stall (2,168ms ->
// 8,225ms) and 26% of the KV pool (11.77M -> 8.70M tokens). The throughput gain is pure
// per-step amortisation and cannot exceed ~7.5% because the per-token cost is flat from 512 to
// 16,384. The KV loss is the sharper cost: it reduces how many conversations keep a warm
// prefix, and a conversation that falls out recomputes its whole context.
//
// CHUNKED PREFILL IS REQUESTED ALONGSIDE IT, because the budget is only meaningful under
// chunking: pinning the budget while inheriting the chunking mode pins half a policy, and a
// future default change would make the pinned number mean something else entirely. This is
// also a no-op today -- the engine already resolves enable_chunked_prefill=True.
//
// THIS ROW IS THE OUTGOING PROFILE'S. The measurements behind it are DeepSeek-V4-Flash on one
// vLLM revision; a different model or image must re-measure rather than inherit the number,
// and must re-establish that the NVML path still resolves the same way.
//
// THE KV DTYPE IS A CORRECTNESS FIELD, NOT A TUNING KNOB, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS BACK. A revision of
// this branch set it to `KvFp8` for GLM-5.3-Flash. `fp8_ds_mla` is the cache layout of DeepSeek's
// Multi-head Latent Attention, and this route serves DeepSeek V4 Flash, so the DeepSeek layout is the
// CORRECT value here -- the GLM change was right about the model it was aimed at and wrong about the
// route it was applied to.
//
// THE GLM REASONING BEHIND THAT CHANGE WAS ALSO PARTLY WRONG, and it is recorded because the
// correction outlived the change. A row asserted GLM-5.3-Flash "is not MLA"; measured on the running
// canary 2026-09-08, the engine selects `FLASHINFER_MLA_SPARSE_SM120` and reports
// "Using fp8_ds_mla KV cache format" FOR GLM. GLM's attention is a hybrid of KDA linear layers and
// NoPE sparse MLA -- so it IS MLA, and the internal cache format is a backend decision downstream of
// the external `--kv-cache-dtype` flag. The launch-wire value and the attention shape are two facts.
//
// THE WINDOW IS DEEPSEEK'S DECLARED CEILING for the same reason. Both models happen to declare
// 1,048,576, which is exactly what made a transcribed literal survive a model change it was never
// about; it is derived from the authority of the model this route actually serves.
// THE SAME 82% THIS ROUTE ALWAYS REQUESTED, NOW IN BASIS POINTS AND IN THE FIELD THE ARGV IS RENDERED
// FROM. 8200 basis points is exactly the integral 82 percent this carried before, so the request is
// unchanged; what changed is that omission is now expressible beside it, which is what lets the
// canary's both-omitted launch live in this same field instead of a parallel carrier.
//
// AND DEEPSEEK REQUESTS PREFIX CACHING EXPLICITLY, WHICH I FIRST RECORDED AS AN OMISSION. Its
// realization renders a literal `--enable-prefix-caching` into the launch line, so `Omitted` would
// have been a model that disagreed with the argv beside it -- the same class of defect this change
// exists to remove, introduced while removing it. The rendered flag is the evidence and it is now
// derived from this field rather than spelled at the launch site.
fn spark_pair_serving_engine_args(tensor_parallel: Nat, max_num_seqs: Nat) -> VllmEngineArgs {
  VllmEngineArgs {
    prefill_scheduling: Present {
      value: VllmPrefillSchedulingPolicy {
        max_num_batched_tokens: token_count(count: 2048),
        chunked_prefill: ChunkedPrefillRequested,
      },
    },
    tensor_parallel_size: tensor_parallel,
    memory_request: VllmMemoryRequest {
      utilization: Present { value: basis_point(count: 8200) },
      kv_cache_bytes: none,
    },
    prefix_caching_request: PrefixCachingExplicit { enabled: true },
    max_model_len: deepseek_v4_context_length,
    max_num_seqs: max_num_seqs,
    kv_cache_dtype: KvFp8DsMla,
    distributed_executor_backend: ExecutorRay,
  }
}

type SparkPairServingParticipant {
  group: FabricGroup
  role: SparkPairServingRole
  host: HostIdentity
  host_ip: Ipv4Address
  lane: FabricLaneObserved
}

type SparkPairServingUnit
  = SparkPairServingUnitReady {
      group: FabricGroup
      engine: ServingEngine
      head: SparkPairServingParticipant
      workers: List<SparkPairServingParticipant>
    }
  | SparkPairServingUnitRefused { group: FabricGroup, cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn enforcer_refusal_wire(cause: EnforcerRefusal) -> String {
  match cause {
    NoEnforcerDeclared => "no memory enforcer declared"
    MoreThanOneEnforcer { count: n } => join(["more than one memory enforcer live: ", to_string(n)], "")
    DeviceReservationCountingEnforcerOnUnifiedPool { kind: k } =>
      join(["a device-reservation-counting enforcer on the unified pool: ", memory_enforcer_kind_wire(kind: k) as String], "")
    UnestablishedReservationAccountingOnUnifiedPool { kind: k } =>
      join(["an enforcer whose device-reservation accounting is unestablished, on the unified pool: ", memory_enforcer_kind_wire(kind: k) as String], "")
  }
}

type PairParticipantResolution
  = ParticipantResolved { participant: SparkPairServingParticipant }
  | ParticipantUnresolved { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn spark_pair_serving_participant(group: FabricGroup, role: SparkPairServingRole, host: HostIdentity) -> PairParticipantResolution {
  match fabric_lane_for_host(host: host) {
    Present { value: o } =>
      ParticipantResolved {
        participant: SparkPairServingParticipant { group: group, role: role, host: host, host_ip: fabric_rail_address_of(o: o), lane: o },
      }
    Absent =>
      ParticipantUnresolved { cause: join([host as String, " is not observed on the switched fabric, so it has no rail address"], "") as NonEmptyStr }
  }
}

type ParticipantsResolution
  = ParticipantsResolved { participants: List<SparkPairServingParticipant> }
  | ParticipantsUnresolved { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn resolution_cause(r: PairParticipantResolution) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match r {
    ParticipantUnresolved { cause: c } => [c]
    ParticipantResolved { participant: _ } => [] as List<NonEmptyStr>
  }
}

fn resolution_participant(r: PairParticipantResolution) -> List<SparkPairServingParticipant> {
  match r {
    ParticipantResolved { participant: p } => [p]
    ParticipantUnresolved { cause: _ } => [] as List<SparkPairServingParticipant>
  }
}

fn spark_pair_serving_workers(group: FabricGroup, hosts: List<HostIdentity>) -> ParticipantsResolution {
  let resolved = map(hosts, h => spark_pair_serving_participant(group: group, role: PairWorker, host: h))
  match first(flat_map(resolved, r => resolution_cause(r: r))) {
    Present { value: c } => ParticipantsUnresolved { cause: c }
    Absent => ParticipantsResolved { participants: flat_map(resolved, r => resolution_participant(r: r)) }
  }
}

fn spark_pair_serving_unit_with(group: FabricGroup, capacity: SparkPairServingSeqCapacity) -> SparkPairServingUnit {
  let hosts = fabric_group_hosts(g: group)
  let reserved = filter(hosts, h => llama_cpp_rpc_host_holds_undischarged_reservation(host: h))
  if length(hosts) < 2 {
    SparkPairServingUnitRefused { group: group, cause: "a serving group needs at least two hosts on the fabric" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else if length(reserved) > 0 {
    SparkPairServingUnitRefused {
      group: group,
      cause: join([
        "group member(s) still hold an undischarged llama.cpp RPC peer reservation, and no release receipt exists: ",
        join(map(reserved, h => h as String), ", "),
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    match capacity {
      SeqCapacityUnmeasured =>
        SparkPairServingUnitRefused { group: group, cause: "max-num-seqs has neither an operator choice nor a measured launch line for this group" as NonEmptyStr }
      SeqCapacityByOperatorPolicy { max_num_seqs: n, basis: _ } =>
        spark_pair_serving_unit_admitted(group: group, hosts: hosts, max_num_seqs: n)
      SeqCapacityMeasured { max_num_seqs: n, basis: _ } =>
        spark_pair_serving_unit_admitted(group: group, hosts: hosts, max_num_seqs: n)
    }
  }
}

// THE TWO ADMITTING ARMS BUILD THE SAME UNIT, so choice-versus-measurement stays a PROVENANCE fact
// rather than becoming two engine tuples free to drift apart.
fn spark_pair_serving_unit_admitted(group: FabricGroup, hosts: List<HostIdentity>, max_num_seqs: Nat) -> SparkPairServingUnit {
  let args = spark_pair_serving_engine_args(tensor_parallel: length(hosts), max_num_seqs: max_num_seqs)
        match spark_memory_enforcer_admits(
          memory_kind: UnifiedShared,
          enforcers: spark_pair_serving_declared_enforcers,
          resource_isolation_enabled: spark_pair_serving_ray_resource_isolation_enabled,
          monitor_refresh: spark_pair_serving_ray_monitor_refresh,
          backend: args.distributed_executor_backend,
        ) {
          EnforcerRefused { cause: c } =>
            SparkPairServingUnitRefused { group: group, cause: join(["memory enforcer law refuses the tuple: ", enforcer_refusal_wire(cause: c)], "") as NonEmptyStr }
          EnforcerAdmitted { enforcer: _ } => {
            let head_host = spark_pair_serving_head(group: group)
            if !any(hosts, h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: head_host)) {
              SparkPairServingUnitRefused { group: group, cause: "the attributed head is not a host of its own fabric group" as NonEmptyStr }
            } else {
              match spark_pair_serving_participant(group: group, role: PairHead, host: head_host) {
                ParticipantUnresolved { cause: c } => SparkPairServingUnitRefused { group: group, cause: c }
                ParticipantResolved { participant: head } =>
                  match spark_pair_serving_workers(group: group, hosts: filter(hosts, h => !host_identity_eq(a: h, b: head_host))) {
                    ParticipantsUnresolved { cause: c } => SparkPairServingUnitRefused { group: group, cause: c }
                    ParticipantsResolved { participants: ws } =>
                      SparkPairServingUnitReady { group: group, engine: VllmServing { args: args }, head: head, workers: ws }
                  }
              }
            }
          }
        }
}

fn spark_pair_serving_unit(group: FabricGroup) -> SparkPairServingUnit {
  spark_pair_serving_unit_with(group: group, capacity: spark_pair_serving_seq_capacity_for(group: group))
}

data spark_pair_serving_desired: List<SparkPairServingUnit> = map(spark_pair_serving_groups, g => spark_pair_serving_unit(group: g))

fn spark_pair_serving_unit_hosts(u: SparkPairServingUnit) -> List<HostIdentity> {
  match u {
    SparkPairServingUnitReady { group: _, engine: _, head: h, workers: ws } => concat([h.host], map(ws, w => w.host))
    SparkPairServingUnitRefused { group: _, cause: _ } => [] as List<HostIdentity>
  }
}

fn spark_pair_serving_desired_hosts() -> List<HostIdentity> {
  flat_map(spark_pair_serving_desired, u => spark_pair_serving_unit_hosts(u: u))
}

fn participant_wire(p: SparkPairServingParticipant) -> String {
  join([p.host as String, "@", render_ipv4_address(addr: p.host_ip) as String], "")
}

fn spark_pair_serving_unit_wire(unit: SparkPairServingUnit) -> String {
  match unit {
    SparkPairServingUnitReady { group: g, engine: _, head: h, workers: ws } =>
      join([
        "group ", fabric_group_wire(g: g) as String,
        " tp=", to_string(1 + length(ws)),
        " head=", participant_wire(p: h),
        " workers=", join(map(ws, w => participant_wire(p: w)), ","),
      ], "")
    SparkPairServingUnitRefused { group: g, cause: c } =>
      join(["group ", fabric_group_wire(g: g) as String, " refused: ", c as String], "")
  }
}
